From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 22:59:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: allow disabling the LE vendor capabilities query

Old Qualcomm WCNSS controllers answer vendor HCI commands with only the OCF,
dropping the OGF. HciLayer matches opcodes strictly and aborts:

  assertion 'waiting_command_ == op_code' failed -
    Waiting for 0xfd57 (LE_ADV_FILTER), got 0x157 (Unknown OpCode: 343)

0xfd57 is OGF 0x3f / OCF 0x157, so the controller echoed the OCF alone. The
stack only reaches LE_ADV_FILTER because LeGetVendorCapabilities (0xfd53)
succeeds and reports filtering support, so it begins issuing APCF commands the
controller cannot round-trip -- a native abort on every Bluetooth enable.

Gated behind bluetooth.core.le.vendor_capabilities.enabled, default true, so
nothing changes for controllers that behave. vendor_capabilities_ is
value-initialised to zero, so skipping the query simply leaves the controller
described as having no vendor extensions and the stack never sends APCF.

Costs offloaded scan filtering and batch scanning on affected devices; the
alternative is no Bluetooth at all.
---
 system/gd/hci/controller.cc | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/system/gd/hci/controller.cc b/system/gd/hci/controller.cc
index 10023e3dd9..bdcc5cc28d 100644
--- a/system/gd/hci/controller.cc
+++ b/system/gd/hci/controller.cc
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 
 #include "hci/controller.h"
 
+#include <android-base/properties.h>
 #include <android-base/strings.h>
 #include <future>
 #include <memory>
@@ -159,8 +160,26 @@ struct Controller::impl {
           handler->BindOnceOn(this, &Controller::impl::le_set_host_feature_handler));
     }
 
-    hci_->EnqueueCommand(LeGetVendorCapabilitiesBuilder::Create(),
-                         handler->BindOnceOn(this, &Controller::impl::le_get_vendor_capabilities_handler));
+      // Querying LE vendor capabilities tells the stack the controller supports
+      // advertising filtering, after which it issues APCF commands. Some older
+      // Qualcomm WCNSS controllers answer a vendor command with only the OCF,
+      // dropping the OGF, and HciLayer matches opcodes strictly:
+      //
+      //   assertion 'waiting_command_ == op_code' failed -
+      //     Waiting for 0xfd57 (LE_ADV_FILTER), got 0x157 (Unknown OpCode: 343)
+      //
+      // 0xfd57 is OGF 0x3f / OCF 0x157, so the controller echoed the OCF alone.
+      // That aborts the whole stack on every enable, making Bluetooth unusable.
+      //
+      // vendor_capabilities_ is value-initialised to all zeroes, so skipping the
+      // query leaves the controller reported as having no vendor extensions and
+      // the stack never sends APCF. Costs offloaded scan filtering and batch
+      // scanning; keeps Bluetooth working.
+      if (android::base::GetBoolProperty(
+              "bluetooth.core.le.vendor_capabilities.enabled", true)) {
+      hci_->EnqueueCommand(LeGetVendorCapabilitiesBuilder::Create(),
+                           handler->BindOnceOn(this, &Controller::impl::le_get_vendor_capabilities_handler));
+      }
 
     // We only need to synchronize the last read. Make BD_ADDR to be the last one.
     std::promise<void> promise;
